Title: A keystore diverging between machines is invisible until a seat dies
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-16T11:35Z claude created
2026-09-16T11:35Z claude note Filed after a rotated OpenRouter key sat on doudou for days while padme kept the dead one. Author asked for the check to be mechanized; the copy itself is already done.
2026-09-23T19:30Z Codex note Roster audit: merged 0393 makes missing reviewer credentials visible at the gate, reducing the silent-failure part of this ticket. It does not compare keystore names or fingerprints across machines. The fleet divergence check and a surviving operational alert remain open; do not count 0393 as this ticket's completion.

--- body ---
## Context

`~/.config/keys/*.env` holds this fleet's credentials, one file per provider,
and each machine keeps its own copy. Nothing compares them. A key rotated on
one machine stays rotated only there until somebody notices — and the way
somebody notices is that a downstream consumer starts failing.

**Observed 2026-09-16.** `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_IDH` on padme returned
`401 User not found`. On doudou the same variable held a different key that
returned `200`, and doudou had already archived the old one as
`EXPIRED_OPENROUTER_API_KEY_IDH`, following the convention the file itself
documents. The rotation was performed correctly; it simply never crossed.

Padme was also missing `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_PI` entirely, which doudou had.
Both have since been copied over and verified (`200` from padme).

## Why this clears the severity floor

The divergence is not what makes this worth mechanizing. The **silence** is.

The consumer here is the `/gaze` external reviewer panel, which is fail-open
by design and by author ruling (2026-09-16): one seat failing must never block
a verdict. That is the right default. Its cost is that a seat with a dead
credential and a seat that reviewed and found nothing are **indistinguishable
in the verdict** — both contribute no findings. The `PANEL-INTEGRITY:` lines
from ticket 0393 are the only surface where the difference shows, and they are
carried as a note.

So the failure mode is: reviews keep running, keep reporting clean, and lose
their external decorrelation entirely, for as long as nobody reads the
integrity lines closely. This is the third instance of the pattern in this
harness:

- **0873** — both CLI reviewer seats silently unauthenticated across *five*
  runs before anyone noticed.
- **0936** — `/gaze` never passed `REVIEWERS_REPO`, so seats on an
  out-of-harness PR died on an unknown pathspec.
- **This one** — a keystore that diverged between machines.

Three different causes, one shape: *an absence of results read as a result*.

## Actions

1. Write a comparison that reports, for each provider file, which variable
   **names** each machine defines and whether the values agree — by
   **fingerprint, never by value**. A sha256 prefix is sufficient and reveals
   nothing usable. The manual version is two commands and was enough to find
   this:

   ```bash
   # names only
   grep -oE '^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=' provider.env | tr -d =
   # agreement, without exposing anything
   env -i bash -c 'set -a; . provider.env; printf %s "$NAME" | sha256sum | cut -c1-12'
   ```

2. Decide where it runs. A cron on one machine cannot see the others without
   reaching into them; the honest options are a check at session start that
   compares against a recorded manifest of expected fingerprints, or an
   explicit `keys-diff <host>` a human runs after a rotation. Prefer the
   second first: it is smaller, it has no standing network requirement, and a
   rotation is already a deliberate act with a natural moment to run it.

3. Separately and more cheaply: make a dead credential **loud where it is
   used**. A seat that fails to authenticate should say so in a way that
   survives fail-open — which `PANEL-INTEGRITY:` already does. The gap is that
   nothing reads those lines but a human. Consider whether `/roar` or
   `/healthcheck` should surface a seat that has not authenticated in N runs,
   which catches the class regardless of *why* the credential died.

Action 3 is worth more than actions 1-2 and should probably lead. A
fingerprint diff catches divergence between machines; a
did-the-seat-authenticate check catches divergence, revocation, quota
exhaustion, and network failure alike, on the surface where it actually
matters.

## Test

Red step: point a seat at a deliberately wrong credential name and confirm
the check reports it. Without that, a check that reports "all agree" on a
healthy fleet proves only that it ran.

The manual diagnosis is the model: the value of the `401` was only
established by a positive control — two sibling keys in the same file
returning `200`, which is what ruled out the account, the network, and the
endpoint in one step.

## Verification

- [ ] A key rotated on one machine and not another is reported, by name, with
      no value or full key material printed anywhere
- [ ] The check itself has a red step: it fails when given a divergence it
      should catch
- [ ] A seat that has not authenticated is visible on a channel something
      other than a human reads

## Invariants

- **No credential value is ever printed, logged, or transmitted** — names and
  fingerprints only. The manual diagnosis that produced this ticket held to
  that and the mechanized version must too.
- Fail-open stays for the panel (author ruling). This ticket makes a dead
  credential *visible*; it does not make it blocking.
- The keystore stays per-machine. Nothing here proposes a shared or synced
  secret store — that is a different decision with a different threat model,
  and it is not being made here by implication.

## Exit criteria

- A credential that works on one machine and not another is reported before a
  consumer silently degrades, or the consumer's degradation is itself
  reported

## Related

- 0873 — seats silently unauthenticated across five runs; same shape
- 0936 — `/gaze` losing its whole panel to a pathspec error; same shape
- 0393 — added the `PANEL-INTEGRITY:` lines, the one surface on which any of
  this is currently visible
